			<div class="content" id="post-content-4844230">
									

<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Genre (Optional):</strong> Horror</p>
<p><strong>Page Layout (Optional):</strong> Standard SCP format with the containment procedures, description, incident log and a log of several SCP-XXXX instances (Possibly collaborative).</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> Incorporeality is a a common power of anomalies. In most cases this is accompanied by some form of levitation. But happened to all those people who have accomplished the former, but not the latter?</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> The SCP is a gigantic mass of incorporeal objects located at the center of the earth, the majority of which are human corpses. The exact size of this mass is [REDACTED] by it is implied to be truly massive. It soon becomes clear to the reader that this is not one central anomaly but the collective resting place of every person or object that has become incorporeal and fallen into the ground. Various examples will include thaumatology, experimental paratechnology, ghosts and possibly a cameo of that D-Class from <a href="/scp-4035">SCP-4035</a>. The document ends with a incident report describing a foundation incorporeal object containment unit (like those mentioned in <a href="/scp-2996">SCP-2996</a> and <a href="/scp-4444">SCP-4444</a>) accidently becoming incorporeal and falling into the mass, causing part of it to become corporal before the unit is destroyed, causing part of the earths core to collapse in on itself, creating a minor earthquake. A notice from a foundation scientist reveals that the mass as a whole has been losing incorporeality overtime, with the eventual mass re-solidification of the anomaly leading to disastrous results.</p>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber:</strong> Taking a semi-used joke to its logical conclusion.</p>
<p><strong>Additional Notes:</strong> I am not sure of a reasonable explanation of the masses re-solidification. Is there an established measure of incorporeal energy that the anomaly can be said to be 'shedding'?</p>

