<p>Thank you for the feedback. Some responses below.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Collapsed&nbsp;for&nbsp;brevity</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Close</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p><em>If something was incorporeal in the core and then became corporeal, why would the core collapse?</em></p>
</blockquote>
<p>It isn't simply more mass being added to the core, it is part of the core being replaced with the incorporeal objects, which are far less dense, allowing large amounts of additional matter to be pushed into the core in a large surge. it should also be noted that due to the nature of several types of intangibility there are several intangible corpse in a single place being expelled from each other while becoming corporeal, potentially causing large seismic disruptions due to the movement of large amounts of mass. As for the 'disastrous' aspect, that is what is being implied to happen in the scenario discussed at the end.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><em>Don't do this, don't redact the size. It doesn't serve anything, do some research and figure out what sorta size you want to imply and just say it. Would be more impactful.</em></p>
</blockquote>
<p>Fair enough, but what more impact would a unit of measure have? I am of the opinion that a well placed redaction can emphasize the horror of an article. To anyone who isn't familiar with the size of a human body or the size of the earths core a definitive amount I feel a definitive amount would lessen the impact. That being said, I may just blackbox the exact volume with a large amount of digits.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>More of concern is the lack of narrative. There isn't an arc to this, its merely discussing the description of an anomaly. What is it you want to say with this article? What do you want the readers to take away from it? We can't really know what the people are going through as they sink to the center of the earth… so where is the human aspect of this anomaly?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You are correct in there not being a central narrative but for an anomaly of this type, there can't really be one, as it isn't the result of one central anomaly. The human aspect of this article comes in the form of the individual stories of the people down there, implied through their descriptions. Furthermore, due to the nature of certain types of intangibility some of the people are still alive and fully aware of there situation, but unable to do anything about it. Given that I am planning on having at least one of the people as a ghost, the foundation may be able to conduct an interview with them via use of a séance. Ending the article with the potential of disaster is meant to convey how this is truly a lose-lose situation, with them being stuck, us being doomed and neither party being able to do anything about it.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

